      "text": "To solve this problem, we first need to calculate the total weight Terrell can lift with the two 20-pound weights. Since he lifts them 12 times, the total weight is 12 * 20.\n\nNow, let's subtract 30 from that total weight as shown:\n\n12 * 20 - 30 = ?\n\nTo calculate the difference, we multiply 12 with 20 and then subtract 30 from the result:\n\n240 - 30 = ?\n\nNow, this difference will be the weight that Terrell must lift with the two 15-pound weights in order to lift the same total weight from before. So:\n\n240 - 30 = 210.\n\nTo find out how many times Terrell must lift the 15-pound weights together, we need to divide 210 by 15:\n\n210 / 15 = ?\n\nFinally, we divide 210 with 15, which is equal to 14.\n\nTherefore,\n\nTerrell must lift the two 15-pound weights 14 times to lift the same total weight that he lifts the two 20-pound weights.\nThus the answer is 14.",
