      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To determine how many times Terrell must lift the 15-pound weights to lift the same total weight as he does with the 20-pound weights, we start by calculating the total weight he lifts in one set of repetitions with the 20-pound weights.\n\nFirst, we calculate the weight lifted in one repetition:\n\\[\n\\text{Weight per repetition} = 2 \\text{ weights} \\times 20 \\text{ pounds/weight} = 40 \\text{ pounds}\n\\]\nNext, we calculate the total weight lifted for one set of 12 repetitions:\n\\[\n\\text{Total weight per set} = 40 \\text{ pounds/repetition} \\times 12 \\text{ repetitions} = 480 \\text{ pounds}\n\\]\n\nNow we need to find out how many sets of 12 repetitions Terrell must perform with the 15-pound weights to lift a total of 480 pounds. Let \\( n \\) be the number of sets he must lift. The total weight lifted in \\( n \\) sets with the 15-pound weights is:\n\\[\n480 \\text{ pounds} = 15 \\text{ pounds/weight} \\times 12 \\text{ repetitions/weight} \\times n\n\\]\nSimplifying, we get:\n\\[\n480 = 180n\n\\]\nSolving for \\( n \\):\n\\[\nn = \\frac{480}{180} = \\frac{8}{3} \\approx 2.67\n\\]\nSince Terrell can't lift a fraction of a set, we round up to the next whole number because even a fraction of a lift requires an additional set to reach the full weight. Therefore, Terrell needs to lift the 15-pound weights:\n\\[\n\\boxed{3}\n\\]\ntimes to lift a total of 480 pounds.",
